BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 588.0 Td (  In the dawn of time, Sol held five golden seeds. He encountered Luna, a spirit of the barren e) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 578.0 Td (    arth. Sol offered the seeds to her, knowing that if Luna buried them in the soil, the Earth-) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 568.0 Td (    Mother would triple their vitality.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 558.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 548.0 Td (  "If you hide them," Sol warned, "they remain stagnant. If you nurture them, they will bloom in) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 538.0 Td (    to a forest.") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 528.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 518.0 Td (  Luna understood the covenant. She received the seeds, and beneath her touch, they surged into ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 508.0 Td (    a thicket of shimmering boughs. Had she been greedy, she might have consumed the harvest alo) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 498.0 Td (    ne, hoarding the fruit for a singular winter. But she knew the cycle of reciprocity. She ret) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 488.0 Td (    urned a bountiful harvest to Sol, ensuring that his storehouse was full for the coming cold.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 478.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 468.0 Td (  The forest grew stronger with every exchange, a testament to the trust between them. When the ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 458.0 Td (    spirits chose to hoard, the land withered, the trees grew brittle and grey. But when they sh) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 448.0 Td (    ared, the bounty multiplied, turning the desert into a paradise. Thus, the law of the harves) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 438.0 Td (    t was written: to grow, one must give, and to receive, one must return. In this cycle, the p) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 428.0 Td (    rosperity of the one is bound to the kindness of the other, forever entwined.) Tj ET
