BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 678.0 Td (      In the Valley of Echoes, two spirits lived on opposite sides of a vast canyon. The Weaver ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 668.0 Td (        held the silver thread, representing the initial gift, while the Merchant guarded the go) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 658.0 Td (        lden loom, which possessed the power to multiply whatever was placed upon it. ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 648.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 638.0 Td (      If the Weaver kept the thread, the canyon remained silent and barren. If the Weaver cast t) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 628.0 Td (        he thread across the chasm, the Merchant would weave it into a brilliant tapestry, tripl) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 618.0 Td (        ing its worth. The Weaver often feared the Merchant might shroud the tapestry in secrecy) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 608.0 Td (        , keeping the entire bounty. Yet, the Merchant understood that if the thread never arriv) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 598.0 Td (        ed, the loom would remain cold and empty.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 588.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 578.0 Td (      One day, the Weaver took a leap of faith and tossed the silver thread. The Merchant, recog) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 568.0 Td (        nizing the sanctity of this offering, did not hoard the silk. Instead, they returned a p) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 558.0 Td (        ortion to the Weaver, honoring the courage it took to trust. They realized that by actin) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 548.0 Td (        g in concert, their prosperity grew beyond the canyon's limits. They held an unspoken pa) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 538.0 Td (        ct: trust is the loom, and reciprocity is the thread. They learned that the greatest ret) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 528.0 Td (        urn lies not in what one keeps, but in the enduring cycle of mutual grace.) Tj ET
