BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 558.0 Td (      In the Valley of Echoes, two spirits lived on opposite sides of a vast canyon. The Weaver ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 548.0 Td (        held the silver thread, representing the initial gift, while the Merchant guarded the go) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 538.0 Td (        lden loom, which possessed the power to multiply whatever was placed upon it. ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 528.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 518.0 Td (      If the Weaver kept the thread, the canyon remained silent and barren. If the Weaver cast t) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 508.0 Td (        he thread across the chasm, the Merchant would weave it into a brilliant tapestry, tripl) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 498.0 Td (        ing its worth. The Weaver often feared the Merchant might shroud the tapestry in secrecy) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 488.0 Td (        , keeping the entire bounty. Yet, the Merchant understood that if the thread never arriv) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 478.0 Td (        ed, the loom would remain cold and empty.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 468.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 458.0 Td (      One day, the Weaver took a leap of faith and tossed the silver thread. The Merchant, recog) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 448.0 Td (        nizing the sanctity of this offering, did not hoard the silk. Instead, they returned a p) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 438.0 Td (        ortion to the Weaver, honoring the courage it took to trust. They realized that by actin) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 428.0 Td (        g in concert, their prosperity grew beyond the canyon's limits. They held an unspoken pa) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 418.0 Td (        ct: trust is the loom, and reciprocity is the thread. They learned that the greatest ret) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 408.0 Td (        urn lies not in what one keeps, but in the enduring cycle of mutual grace.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 398.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 388.0 Td (      Here is the myth the other agent wrote in the previous round:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 378.0 Td (      In the beginning, there was only the dry soil of the Void. Two spirits, Aethel and Solis, ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 368.0 Td (        held seeds of life. Aethel chose to keep hers, fearful of the shifting winds. Her seeds ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 358.0 Td (        remained stagnant, gray pebbles in a locked chest. Solis, however, walked to the edge of) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 348.0 Td (         the chasm and cast his seeds across the expanse to Aethel. As they traveled, the air tr) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 338.0 Td (        ansformed them, tripling their potential through the magic of the chasm's breath. ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 328.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 318.0 Td (      Aethel caught the bounty. She understood the weight of the gift. Recognizing the spirit of) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 308.0 Td (         trust, she returned a portion of the harvest back to Solis. The return was not just a t) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 298.0 Td (        ransaction; it was a weave, binding their fates. Where once there was dust, a forest gre) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 288.0 Td (        w, fed by the constant cycle of giving and receiving. The spirits learned that holding o) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 278.0 Td (        nto one's grain meant starving, but pouring it into the hands of another created a harve) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 268.0 Td (        st that neither could achieve alone. In this realm, the multiplier of trust is the only ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 258.0 Td (        currency that yields a garden. To hold is to wither; to release is to flourish. Thus, th) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 248.0 Td (        e cycle of the chasm became the law of the cosmos: give freely, return justly, and watch) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 238.0 Td (         the world bloom.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 228.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
